## 8. Proposed Business Process

1. The doctor conducts the patient consultation.
2. Authorized consultation information is provided to the AI-assisted system.
3. AI generates a draft clinical note.
4. The scribe reviews the draft and corrects documentation mistakes.
5. The doctor reviews the corrected note and checks clinical accuracy.
6. If further corrections are required, the note is returned through the agreed correction workflow.
7. The doctor approves the note.
8. The approved note is finalized or saved to the correct EHR record through an authorized process.

## 9. Business Success Metrics

The following indicators can be used to evaluate the proposed solution:

- Average time required to prepare a clinical note.
- Average time spent by the scribe correcting a draft.
- Percentage of notes requiring significant corrections.
- Percentage of required clinical note sections completed.
- Time from draft generation to doctor approval.
- Number of incomplete or pending clinical notes.

Baseline values and target improvements must be agreed upon with stakeholders. This conceptual project does not claim measured results.

## 10. Assumptions and Dependencies

- The organization authorizes the proposed AI-assisted documentation process.
- Consultation information can be provided through an approved input method.
- AI can generate a draft for human review.
- The scribe can review and correct the draft.
- The doctor can review and approve the corrected note.
- EHR integration or an authorized transfer method is technically feasible.
- Applicable privacy, security, consent, and record-retention requirements are addressed.

## 11. Risks and Constraints

| Risk                                          | Business Impact                                | Proposed Mitigation                                             |
| --------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------- |
| AI generates inaccurate information           | Incorrect content may enter the draft          | Require scribe review and doctor approval                       |
| Required information is missing               | The note may be incomplete                     | Identify missing information for review                         |
| Incorrect patient or encounter is selected    | Documentation may be saved to the wrong record | Verify patient and encounter before saving                      |
| AI generation or EHR saving fails             | Documentation may be delayed                   | Display failure status and provide an approved recovery process |
| Unauthorized access to patient information    | Privacy and security risk                      | Apply approved access controls and auditing                     |
| Users do not understand the proposed workflow | Delays or inconsistent use                     | Provide workflow guidance and appropriate training              |

## 12. Business Acceptance Criteria

The proposed solution will be considered ready for business acceptance when:

1. Business requirements have been reviewed with relevant stakeholders.
2. The system generates a structured draft from authorized consultation information.
3. The scribe can review and correct the draft.
4. The doctor can review the corrected note and approve it or request further corrections.
5. Required doctor approval is enforced before finalization.
6. The correct patient and encounter are verified before saving.
7. Saving failures are handled without falsely reporting success.
8. Privacy, security, and access-control requirements have been reviewed.
9. Agreed UAT scenarios have been executed and results documented.

## 13. Approval and Document Status

This document is a draft for an educational Business Analyst portfolio project. The proposed workflow and requirements have not been validated through formal stakeholder interviews or production testing. Stakeholder review and approval would be required before implementation in a real healthcare environment.
